      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let the unknown pressure be \\( x \\). Since the pressure is inversely proportional to the volume, we can set up the equation \\( \\dfrac {5 \\text { kPa}}{2.28 \\text { liter}} = \\dfrac {x}{5.7 \\text { liter}} \\). Cross multiplying gives us \\( 5 \\times 5.7 = 2.28 \\times x \\). Solving for \\( x \\) results in \\( x = \\dfrac {5 \\times 5.7}{2.28} = \\dfrac {28.5}{2.28} \\ldots $ This means the command is to compute \\( \\dfrac {28.5}{2.28} \\) and put the answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\nThe given relationship is that the pressure \\( P \\) of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume \\( V \\). Mathematically, this can be expressed as:\n\\[ P_1 V_1 = P_2 V_2 \\]\nwhere \\( P_1 \\) and \\( V_1 \\) are the initial pressure and volume, and \\( P_2 \\) and \\( V_2 \\) are the final pressure and volume.\n\nWe are given:\n\\[ P_1 = 5 \\text{ kPa} \\]\n\\[ V_1 = 2.28 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\\[ V_2 = 5.7 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( P_2 \\). Using the inverse proportionality relationship, we can write:\n\\[ P_1 V_1 = P_2 V_2 \\]\nSubstituting the given values into the equation, we get:\n\\[ 5 \\text{ kPa} \\times 2.28 \\text{ liters} = P_2 \\times 5.7 \\text{ liters} \\]\n\nTo find \\( P_2 \\), we solve for \\( P_2 \\) by isolating it on one side of the equation:\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{5 \\text{ kPa} \\times 2.28 \\text{ liters}}{5.7 \\text{ liters}} \\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication and division:\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4 \\text{ kPa} \\cdot \\text{liters}}{5.7 \\text{ liters}} = \\frac{11.4}{5.7} \\text{ kPa} \\approx 2.0 \\text{ kPa} \\]\n\nTherefore, the new pressure \\( P_2 \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2.0} \\]",
